<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#slavery-in-the-aztec-empire">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slavery in the Aztec Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as tlacotli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> and the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> societies was widespread; a slave was called a tlacotli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>. Slaves did not inherit their status, and their children were set free: people were enslaved as a punishment, as captives of war, or voluntarily to pay debts, among them gambling debts, and during a famine in the 1450s many sold themselves into slavery. Most served in the palaces of lords; they could marry and own property, and owners had to feed and shelter them. War captives fared worst, being sold or sacrificed at religious festivals. Slaves were sold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and above all at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a> and Itzocan, those judged incorrigible wearing wooden collars.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Enslaved people (<b>tlacotin</b>) were owned by others, but Aztec slavery was not inherited: the children of slaves were born free.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
